= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $\mathbb H=\{z:\operatorname{Im}z>0\}$. A <compact H-hull> is a bounded, relatively closed set $A\subseteq\mathbb H$ for which $D_A=\mathbb H\setminus A$ is a <simply connected domain>. “Compact” refers to its compact <closure> in $\overline{\mathbb H}$; <boundary> points on $\mathbb R$ can be adjoined without changing the remaining <domain>.

The <Riemann mapping theorem> and normalization at infinity give a unique <mapping-out function of a compact H-hull> $g_A:D_A\to\mathbb H$ with <hydrodynamic normalization at infinity>. By the <Schwarz reflection principle> outside a large disc, it has the <Laurent series>
$$
g_A(z)=z+\frac{a}{z}+O(|z|^{-2}),\qquad a\in\mathbb R.
$$
The <half-plane capacity> is the coefficient
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{hcap}(A)=a.}
$$
The empty <compact H-hull> has $g_A(z)=z$ and <half-plane capacity> zero. Nonnegativity follows from the <Brownian representation of half-plane capacity> proved next.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $u(z)=\operatorname{Im}(z-g_A(z))$ on $D_A$. This is a <harmonic function>, tends to zero at infinity, and is bounded. To justify the <boundary> behavior without requiring a <locally connected space> as its <boundary>, let $f_A=g_A^{-1}$. Its expansion at infinity implies that bounded $z$ cannot have $|g_A(z)|\to\infty$. If $z_n$ approaches a finite point of $\partial D_A$ and $g_A(z_n)$ had a subsequential limit inside $\mathbb H$, <continuity> of $f_A$ would force that point to lie inside $D_A$, a contradiction. Thus <boundary degeneration under a mapping-out function> gives $\operatorname{Im}g_A(z_n)\to0$. Consequently $u$ extends continuously to the finite <boundary> with value $\operatorname{Im}z$.

Let $B$ be <planar Brownian motion> started at $z\in D_A$, and $\tau$ its <Brownian exit time> from $D_A$. This time is finite almost surely: it is at most the first time the imaginary coordinate, a one-dimensional <Brownian motion>, hits zero. By the <Itô formula>, $u(B_{t\wedge\tau})$ is a bounded <martingale>. The <optional stopping theorem> and <dominated convergence theorem> therefore give
$$
u(z)=\mathbb E_z[u(B_\tau)]=\mathbb E_z[\operatorname{Im}B_\tau].
$$
Boundedness is important here: directly stopping the unbounded imaginary-coordinate <martingale> would require an unjustified <uniform integrability> assertion.

At $z=iy$, the <Laurent series> gives $u(iy)=a/y+O(y^{-2})$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{hcap}(A)=\lim_{y\to\infty}y\,\mathbb E_{iy}[\operatorname{Im}B_\tau]\geq0.}
$$
The sign follows because the exit point lies on $\partial D_A\subseteq\overline{\mathbb H}$. This proves the <Brownian representation of half-plane capacity>.

